{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "43",
    "case_summary": "A 7-month-old female infant from a Sudanese western tribe was referred with concerns of malnutrition. Upon examination, the patient exhibited confluent, arched, and well-defined eyebrows, long curly eyelashes, low front and back hairlines, an upturned nose, down-turned corners of the mouth, thin lips, a long philtrum, a small lower jaw, and a protruding upper jaw. Additional findings included microcephaly, excessive body hair, a short neck, small broad hands with a simian crease, proximal insertion of the thumb, and clinodactyly of the fifth finger. The patient had a low-pitched cry, a short neck with limited movement, stiff muscle tone (unable to sit, still fisting), fixed flexion of both elbows, and no signs of speaking or babbling. Other observations included a tied tongue and thin skin. The patient's length was approximately 27 inches, weight was 3 kilograms, and skull circumference was 35 cm. An X-ray revealed crowded ribs with short distances between each rib. Peripheral blood was cultured in RPMI 1640 medium for 72 hours, followed by metaphase arrest using Colcemide. Cells were harvested, treated with a hypotonic solution, and fixed with methanol and acetic acid. The cells were then dropped on clean slides and stained with Wright's stain for chromosome banding. Clonality criteria and karyotypic descriptions followed ISCN recommendations.\n\nX-ray showed delayed bone development. Her brain stem evoked potential test showed conductive hearing loss. Hepatomegally was shown by ultrasonography. Analysis of 11 metaphase cells showed 46, XX in all cells.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
